[{:seon.task/name "a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29 (docs/decisions.md, config at every level, no env var switches): a setting can be set at every level, in this order, later wins: shipped defaults < profile (--profile) < --config FILE < --set < main's root entity < model < provider < task < agent; and each key's schema names the levels it may be set at. What exists: `:seon.task/config` is a closed map of six config keys (seon.task.edn:17), a second list beside the schemas; `:seon.config/place` marks per-process keys (fx.clj:267); nothing says which keys may be set at runtime, per model, per task or per agent. Change: schema entries carry `:seon.config/levels`, a subset of #{:boot :runtime :model :provider :task :agent} (default #{:boot}: shipped, profile, --config, --set); `:seon.task/config`'s and the agent's keys are derived from the keys whose levels have :task / :agent (the six get :task); `seon.config/resolve-in` and task declaration refuse a key set at a level its schema does not name, with an error naming key, level and the levels it has; the config panel shows each key's levels; docs/config.md gets a table of keys by level generated by the same read. Replaces the hand list in seon.task.edn. Proof: a task declaring a :boot-only key is refused; a :task key resolves with source [:task]; every key in the six-key list has :task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-key-set-at-a-level-its-schema-excludes-is-refused seon.config-test/every-task-config-key-has-the-task-level}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `cfg/value` (121 call sites) reads the boot map; only cfg/provider-at (turn.clj:290) merges `from-db` main, so a change to main's root entity applies to the provider alone, though the ruling puts main's root entity above --set and below the model. Change: `resolve-in` takes the config in force as `(merge config (from-db main))` keyed by main's commit (a reference, not a held db: AGENTS 4), so the runtime layer applies to every key whose levels have :runtime, with source [:main]; `sources` names it; the three `(get cfg/config k)` bypasses (root.clj:958,964; fork_server.clj:173,192; my/root.clj:294) go through `cfg/value`. Measure: calls per turn and bytes of a resolve before/after (a cost proof, work not time). Deletes provider-at's own merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/a-keys-levels-are-its-schemas"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/a-runtime-change-on-main-applies-to-the-next-read seon.config-test/provider-at-is-the-resolver}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29: the agent is a level beside the task, above it (config 'added to an agent's entity to override on a per agent level'). What exists: an agent is a forked process running one task; its identity for the resolver is the task id, and no level is its own. Change: ctx gains `:agent`; `resolve-in` gets one clause above the task's, reading the declared :seon.config.* attributes of the agent's run entity whose key names :agent in its levels (the same flattening task.clj:614 does for a task); `(my.root/run! sys [id] {:config {...}})` writes them; `sources` reports [:agent]. Reuses the one read; adds one clause and no attribute type. Proof: two runs of one task with different :seon.config.ai/model each use theirs, and a key without :agent in its levels is refused there."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/the-resolver-reads-main-s-copy"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/an-agent-s-setting-wins-over-its-task-s seon.root-test/two-runs-of-a-task-use-their-own-model}}
 {:seon.task/name "per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner ruling 2026-10-29: bin/ passes --set, not env vars. What exists: bin/test, bin/check, bin/root, bin/env and test_store.clj set SEON_WIKI_BASELINE, RUNS, STORE, MAIN, SOURCE_ROOT, RESULTS_DIR, COST_MAX_N, SEEDS, EMBED_TEXTS, WEB_PORT, SERVER_PORT, INSTRUMENT, AI_MODEL, RUN_CHECKOUT, which default.edn reads with `#env` under --set (the stale-local.edn failure docs/config.md already names); fx.clj:545,756-757 strips the leaked ones from test children; config/local.edn holds two `#env`. Change: delete the remaining `#env` tags and local.edn's two (bin/ports writes plain port values); bin/ scripts and test_store pass `--set`, as they already pass `--profile`; one function `seon.config/args` builds a child's `--set` list from the keys marked `:seon.config/place`; the strip lists keep only launcher variables; the no-env-tag test's exception set (SEON_AI_MODEL, SEON_INSTRUMENT) empties and the test becomes `default.edn reads no environment`. Measure: verdict reach digests move once (state it), `bin/check config` green. Deletes: 14 env exports, the strip entries, config.md's warning."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/default-edn-reads-no-environment seon.config-test/a-child-gets-its-keys-as-set-arguments seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-plain-ports}}
 {:seon.task/name "config-page-lists-levels-env-and-process-knobs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/config.md has layers, the profile and the resolver but no table of levels, of the environment variables that stay (SEON_CHECKOUT, the API-key variable, launcher JOLT_*/HOME), or of process knobs (machine.md has the heap ones). Change, finished here after the tasks above: one 'Levels' table (the ruling's order, each level's source name), one 'Environment' table (each remaining variable, who reads it, why), one 'Process settings' table (JOLT_MAX_HEAP/NEW_SIZE, MAX_HEAP_FREE_RATIO, sweepers, COMPILER_OPTIONS, native libs: key, edge function, process, when), linked from machine.md, model.md (SEON_AI_MODEL becomes --set) and the default.edn header. bin/root drops its repeated JOLT_*_LIB line by sourcing bin/env with the tree's ROOT. Proof: a test reads each table row's key and edge function and checks it exists."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.config/per-run-keys-ride-the-command-line" "seon.config/an-agent-can-carry-its-own-config"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/every-process-knob-names-its-key-and-edge-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "agents-at-once-follow-the-machine"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-29: \"10-20 should be fine for users with small machines and the sky is the limit for servers and big iron\". :seon.config.agent/max-running was 4 (v1's value for a 16 GB machine when an agent cost ~100 MB by footprint); bin/check scale measured an agent's real cost at 20.6 MB (the machine's drop in free plus inactive, 100 agents, 2026-10-29). Correct: the default is 10, and root computes the number from the machine (total RAM by sysctl hw.memsize through seon.lib, :seon.config.machine/memory-mb's share for Seon, root and the fork server's footprint, an agent's measured cost with headroom for its test children), at least 10 on an 8 GB machine, no fixed ceiling above; an explicit --set wins. One resolver (seon.config), the value recorded on main's run fact as now. Proofs: the computed number on 8, 16 and 128 GB inputs; bin/check scale at the computed number."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
